WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.610
Arby’s.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.090
R. B.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.500
Roast beef.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.840
That sounds like a mystery solved.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.720
Except those letters
stand for something else.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.410
And once you learn the stories
behind these four restaurant names,

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.020
you might never read their
signs quite the same way again.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.110
Let’s start with the sandwich.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.180
Picture a roast-beef sandwich
beside two enormous letters.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.580
R and B.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.110
Say them together.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.290
“Arby.”

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.940
It’s such a convincing explanation
that you barely have to think about it.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.018
But according to Arby’s parent company,
the name refers to the Raffel Brothers:

00:00:37.058 --> 00:00:38.580
Leroy and Forrest.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:43.450
They founded the restaurant
in Boardman, Ohio, in 1964.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.210
Raffel Brothers.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.840
R. B.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.200
Arby’s.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.790
The initials belong to the founders.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:55.250
The roast beef makes the coincidence
especially easy to believe.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.190
That’s what makes this
a good naming mystery.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.550
The answer seems to be sitting
right there on the menu.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.570
But the real explanation is
standing behind the counter.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.876
Two brothers whose initials became a
name millions of customers would say when

00:01:10.916 --> 00:01:12.220
ordering lunch.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.240
Now let’s move from two
brothers to one daughter.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.190
Who was Wendy?

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.120
If your answer is “the founder’s
daughter,” you’re on the right track.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.130
But there’s another detail.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.130
Her name was Melinda.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.844
Dave Thomas considered his children’s
names before choosing Melinda’s nickname

00:01:30.884 --> 00:01:32.360
for the restaurant.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:38.545
Wendy’s official history explains that her
siblings had trouble pronouncing her name.

00:01:38.585 --> 00:01:41.060
“Wenda” eventually became “Wendy.”

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.250
A family nickname became
a restaurant name.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.650
Imagine that transformation.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.360
At home, it’s a name your
brothers and sisters call you.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.730
Outside, it becomes a
sign above a restaurant.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.724
Then customers start saying it
without knowing anything about the

00:01:57.764 --> 00:01:59.100
family behind it.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.740
The name sounds like a person
because it came from one.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.820
But the story sits one layer
beneath the obvious answer.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.550
Wendy was real.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.950
Wendy was also a nickname.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.760
Now for a name with something
you can actually picture.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.820
You hear “Taco Bell,” and the words
seem perfectly matched to the logo.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.760
A taco restaurant.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.030
A bell.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.330
But the Bell is also a person.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.990
Glen Bell founded the chain, opening
its first restaurant in Downey,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.660
California, in 1962.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:36.300
His surname is right there in the brand.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.390
Once you know that, the
name reads differently.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.470
It isn’t simply a food
followed by a familiar object.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.100
It’s the food followed by the founder.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.640
The bell symbol makes the
name easy to visualize.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.790
But the family name
gives it another meaning.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.890
We’ve now found brothers,
a daughter’s nickname,

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.350
and a founder hiding in plain sight.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.380
Our last stop takes us
somewhere quite different.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.220
A coffee shop name that
leads to a map—and a novel.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.380
Before we reveal this one, take a guess.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.040
Was Starbucks named after a founder?

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.750
A type of coffee?

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.710
Or a character in a book?

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.990
The book is part of the answer.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.572
Starbucks’ own account describes a naming
process that included the discovery of

00:03:23.612 --> 00:03:25.430
“Starbo” on an old map.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.537
That brought the team back to Starbuck—the
first mate aboard the Pequod in Herman

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:32.040
Melville’s Moby-Dick.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:37.140
The name we associate with coffee has
a literary and seafaring connection.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:41.530
It wasn’t simply a matter of opening
a book and pointing at a page.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.840
The sound of a place name helped
lead them toward a character’s name.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.090
Picture the journey on screen.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.630
An old map.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.420
The word “Starbo.”

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.030
A book opening.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.460
“Starbuck.”

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.910
Then the familiar coffee-shop name.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.480
A surprising route to something we
now read without a second thought.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.760
Four familiar names.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.280
Two brothers.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.080
A family nickname.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.890
A founder’s surname.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.490
And a trail from an old
map to a literary sailor.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.810
You don’t need to know any of
those stories to order lunch.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.560
But knowing them changes what you notice.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.290
Next time you pass one of those signs,
you’ll see more than a restaurant name.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.970
You’ll see a little piece of how it began.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.340
Which story surprised you most?

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.750
And what company name
should we investigate next?

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.610
Now you know.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.710
Ordinary has secrets.
